id: core/turn
title: "turn and causal time"
kind: temporal-contract
status: draft-0.2
updated: 2026-08-13

definition: >
  A turn is a bounded causally coherent episode opened against a state. An
  agent may act, communicate, delegate, wait, or yield. The turn remains open
  until an explicit outcome, handoff, abandonment, or supersession closes the
  causal loop.

distinctions:
  choice: "an attributed selection among alternatives"
  move: "an intended and bounded commitment or deliberate non-action"
  event: "an attributed occurrence, including exogenous change"
  transition: "the measured state or affordance delta; not the move itself"
  proof_receipt: "evidence evaluated against a prior proof contract"
  update: "state materialized from authorized events"

lifecycle:
  - open
  - active
  - waiting
  - blocked
  - delegated
  - resumed
  - closed
  - abandoned
  - superseded

waiting_fields:
  - waiting_on
  - since
  - resume_when
  - deadline
  - owner

causality:
  total_global_order_required: false
  overlap_allowed: true
  nesting_allowed: true
  identifiers:
    - parent_turn_id
    - caused_by_event_ids
    - correlation_id
    - local_event_cursor

invariants:
  - "Reality is not claimed to literally alternate turns."
  - "Waiting is a first-class state, not a missing trace."
  - "A move cannot verify its own outcome."
  - "An execution may suspend while the causal turn remains open."
  - "Delegation narrows context and authority unless a rightful grant says otherwise."
  - "Timeout alone does not imply failure."

links:
  - spec/turns.md
  - core/state.yawn
  - core/state-transition.yawn
  - core/event-reduction.yawn
  - templates/turn.yawn
